{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityCountingBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityCountingBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.AbsoluteValue.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityCountingBound.abs_sum_le_card_mul_dominant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityCountingBound.abs_sum_le_card_mul_dominant","kind":"theorem","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityCountingBound.lean#L53-L58","doc":"The same bound stated against the dominant summand: if `a i₀` attains the\nmaximum absolute value, the whole sum is at most `card` times it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityCountingBound.abs_sum_le_card_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityCountingBound.abs_sum_le_card_mul","kind":"theorem","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityCountingBound.lean#L43-L51","doc":"**Many summands buy at most a factor of their number.** A signed sum whose\nsummands are bounded by `M` is bounded by `card · M`. This is the entire\nmultiplicity channel: the triangle inequality, and no more is available. ","signatureChunk":0}]}